Subject: `new` reads an instantiated bytecode constructor's `prototype` directly

What this does
  `new` reads an instantiated bytecode constructor's `prototype` directly.

Why we need it
  `js_create_from_ctor_cap` read `ctor.prototype` with `JS_GetProperty` on every `new`: a shape walk, a cache probe and a getter test for what is always an own plain slot.

What it adds
  When the receiver is a bytecode function whose own `prototype` is present and `JS_PROP_NORMAL`, take `js_dup(pr->u.value)` as the result -- which is what `JS_GetProperty` would return, since a constructor's prototype is never an accessor (the property is non-configurable, `JS_PROP_WRITABLE`). Every other case keeps the generic read: not found, the `JS_PROP_AUTOINIT` stub an uninstantiated function carries (the read is what realises it), a getter, a var_ref, or any other class. `QJS_CTOR_PROTO_FAST=0` restores the generic read.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.
diff --git a/quickjs.c b/quickjs.c
